+### AppProtocol
+
+The `AppProtocol` matcher allows matching connections on the application protocol
+detected **after** TLS termination, by looking at the first bytes of the decrypted
+stream. It makes it possible to serve a regular HTTPS site on an entryPoint, and to
+send the connections carrying anything else to a TCP service.
+
+Accepted values are `http/1.1`, `h2` and `unknown`. Any other value makes the router
+error out.
+
+A router using this matcher (below, a *post-TLS router*) is subject to the following
+constraints:
+
+| Field            | Requirement                                                          |
+|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------|
+| <a id="opt-tls" href="#opt-tls" title="#opt-tls">`tls`</a> | Must be set: the matcher works on the decrypted stream.               |
+| <a id="opt-tls-passthrough" href="#opt-tls-passthrough" title="#opt-tls-passthrough">`tls.passthrough`</a> | Must be `false`.                                                      |
+| <a id="opt-tls-options" href="#opt-tls-options" title="#opt-tls-options">`tls.options`</a> | Must not be set, see the note below.                                  |
+| <a id="opt-Rule-syntax" href="#opt-Rule-syntax" title="#opt-Rule-syntax">Rule syntax</a> | Must be v3 (the default).                                             |
+
+!!! info "TLS configuration"
+
+    The TLS configuration of such a connection is decided by the HTTPS path it went
+    through: the TLS options resolved for the HTTP router with the same SNI, or the
+    entryPoint default ones when there is no such router. Certificates are still
+    picked from the certificate store by SNI, as usual. Setting `tls.options` on a
+    post-TLS router is therefore rejected.
+
+Post-TLS routers only compete with each other, they never take precedence over the
+regular HTTPS traffic of the entryPoint. A connection already claimed at the
+ClientHello stage by a TCP-TLS router (including a passthrough one) never reaches
+this second stage.
+
+#### Detection
+
+The detection branches on the ALPN protocol negotiated during the handshake, because
+that result is binding for the connection — this is exactly what Traefik's HTTP
+server does with a connection that is not sniffed:
+
+- **`h2` negotiated**: `h2` when the payload starts with the 24 bytes
+  `PRI * HTTP/2.0\r\n\r\nSM\r\n\r\n`, `unknown` otherwise.
+- **`http/1.1` or no protocol negotiated**: `http/1.1` when the payload looks like a
+  request line (see below), `unknown` otherwise.
+- **Any other protocol**: not sniffed, always `unknown`.
+
+!!! tip "Make the detection exact"
+
+    On a connection that negotiated `h2`, the payload is `unknown` as soon as it
+    differs from those 24 bytes, which makes the decision exact. Have the fallback
+    client offer `h2` in its ALPN extension to benefit from it. Note that ALPN
+    negotiation is server-order-first and that Traefik offers `["h2", "http/1.1",
+    "acme-tls/1"]`, so a client offering `h2` always ends up negotiating it,
+    whatever its own ordering. As a bonus, this is also what a browser does.
+
+On the `http/1.1` branch, the payload is taken for HTTP when it looks like
+`<token>{1,32} SP <VCHAR>`, which is what Go's HTTP server requires to accept a
+request line. This is deliberately **not** a method allow-list: RFC 9110 defines
+`extension-method` as a bare token, so `QUERY`, the WebDAV methods (`PROPFIND`,
+`PROPPATCH`, `MKCOL`, `COPY`, `MOVE`, `LOCK`, `UNLOCK`), the CalDAV ones (`REPORT`,
+`MKCALENDAR`), and any custom method are all correctly detected as HTTP and never
+sent to the fallback service.
+
+- token characters: ``!#$%&'*+-.^_`|~`` + `0-9` + `A-Z` + `a-z`
+- VCHAR: `0x21`–`0x7E`
+- method name length limit: 32 (the longest IANA-registered method,
+  `UPDATEREDIRECTREF`, is 17 characters)
+
+The guarantee only holds one way: a payload detected as `unknown` is one Traefik's
+HTTP server would have rejected anyway, but a binary payload may well be detected as
+`http/1.1`, in which case the HTTP server answers `400` and closes the connection.
+Check the header bytes of your protocol against the rule above before configuring a
+fallback service.
+
+A connection that does not send enough bytes to be decided on within 500ms (which
+covers a browser opening a connection ahead of time, and any protocol where the
+server speaks first) takes no part in the matching and goes down the regular HTTPS
+path.
+
+!!! warning "Known behavior difference"
+
+    On an entryPoint carrying at least one post-TLS router, a connection that
+    negotiated `h2`, whose payload is not the HTTP/2 preface, and which no post-TLS
+    router matches, is served as HTTP/1.x instead of being closed by the HTTP/2
+    server. This only happens when a client contradicts the ALPN protocol it
+    negotiated itself.
+
+#### Combining with the other matchers
+
+`HostSNI`, `HostSNIRegexp`, `ClientIP` and `ALPN` can all be combined with
+`AppProtocol`, with the exact same semantics they have anywhere else. In particular,
+`ALPN` still matches the protocols **offered** by the client in the ClientHello, not
+the negotiated one.
+
+```yaml
+# The client claims h2, but what it sends is not: hand it to the fallback service.
+HostSNI(`example.com`) && ALPN(`h2`) && AppProtocol(`unknown`)
+```
+
+#### Examples
+
+Serve `example.com` over HTTPS, and send everything else on that connection to an
+internal TCP service:
+
+```yaml tab="Structured (YAML)"
+tcp:
+  routers:
+    camouflage-fallback:
+      entryPoints:
+        - websecure
+      rule: "HostSNI(`example.com`) && AppProtocol(`unknown`)"
+      service: inner-proxy
+      tls: {}
+
+  services:
+    inner-proxy:
+      loadBalancer:
+        servers:
+          - address: "127.0.0.1:10000"
+
+http:
+  routers:
+    site:
+      entryPoints:
+        - websecure
+      rule: "Host(`example.com`)"
+      service: site
+      tls: {}
+```
+
+```toml tab="Structured (TOML)"
+[tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback]
+  entryPoints = ["websecure"]
+  rule = "HostSNI(`example.com`) && AppProtocol(`unknown`)"
+  service = "inner-proxy"
+  [tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback.tls]
+
+[tcp.services.inner-proxy.loadBalancer]
+  [[tcp.services.inner-proxy.loadBalancer.servers]]
+    address = "127.0.0.1:10000"
+
+[http.routers.site]
+  entryPoints = ["websecure"]
+  rule = "Host(`example.com`)"
+  service = "site"
+  [http.routers.site.tls]
+```
+
+```yaml tab="Labels"
+labels:
+  - 'traefik.tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback.rule=HostSNI(`example.com`) && AppProtocol(`unknown`)'
+  - "traefik.tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback.entryPoints=websecure"
+  - "traefik.tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback.service=inner-proxy"
+  - "traefik.tcp.routers.camouflage-fallback.tls=true"
+```
+
+```yaml tab="Kubernetes"
+apiVersion: traefik.io/v1alpha1
+kind: IngressRouteTCP
+metadata:
+  name: camouflage-fallback
+spec:
+  entryPoints:
+    - websecure
+  routes:
+    - match: HostSNI(`example.com`) && AppProtocol(`unknown`)
+      services:
+        - name: inner-proxy
+          port: 10000
+  tls: {}
+```
+
